denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510385 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510385 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510385 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510385 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x+{y}^{2}},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510385 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41ff97cf-642e-5207-833e-62b8b292b55c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510385 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+{y}^{2}})={e}^{x+{y}^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+{y}^{2}})=2y{e}^{x+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{x+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=1,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-1=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n1+1+2=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510445 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510445 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510445 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510445 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ 2 & ∗\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc54e1e5-dbca-5fc5-bf13-79bd1c80e680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510445 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})=\\frac{2}{2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})=\\frac{1}{2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2})=2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2})=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}} & \\frac{1}{2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+2+5=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510464 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510464 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510464 #edb/ref 1071 "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y-x \\\\ {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {x}^{2}+y\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ 2x & \\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510464 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4c8c472-15d1-572f-9440-48aac06cd29f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510464 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-x)=-1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-x)=1, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})={e}^{x+y},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y})={e}^{x+y}, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ 2x & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, the missing entries are $1,\\,$ ${e}^{x+y},$ and $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510466 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510466 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510466 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510466 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e574592a-62af-5f54-90e2-87d7393db0a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510466 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}})={x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}})={e}^{{x}_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3{x}_{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3{x}_{2})=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}} & {e}^{{x}_{1}} \\\\ 0 & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡6\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡6+0+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6=\\operatorname{sin}⁡6+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
